    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d273">

                <pb facs="0393" n="283" sameAs="frus:frus1931v03/pg283"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">793.94/2463</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Military Attaché in Japan</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">McIlroy</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Adjutant
                        General of the Army</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Bridges</hi>)<anchor n="4"
                        xml:id="d273-f1"/></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Tokyo</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1931-10-21">October 21, 1931</date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p>No. 190. Full General Tanaka,<anchor n="*" xml:id="d273-f2"/> Retired, calling at
                    my house last night by appointment stated very emphatically “I ask you to cable
                    your Secretary of War that the fighting men of Japan will not permit the League
                    of Nations or America to intervene in any way that might weaken Japan’s position
                    in Manchuria.”</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">MoIlroy</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d273-f1" n="4" type="source" xml:id="d273-f1-content">
                <p>Copy of radiogram transmitted to the Department of State by the War Department,
                    October 21.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d273-f2" n="*" xml:id="d273-f2-content">
                <p>Unable to identify definitely. Believed to be General Kunishige Tanaka who was
                    Military Attaché in Washington &amp; London, aide-de-camp to the Emperor, headed
                    military delegates to Washington Conference. WCC. (Major Crane) [Footnote in the
                    original by Maj. W. C. Crane.]</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
